#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088312 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x + 5)^{2} + (y + 1)^{2} = 4$?" :question/worked-solution "Compare $(x+5)^2+(y+1)^2=4$ with $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$. The center is $(-5,-1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt4=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088321 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088322 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{10} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Compare with $\\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1$. Here $a=\\sqrt{10}$ and $b=3$, with $a>b$. The lengths of the major and minor axes are therefore\n\n$$\n2a=2\\sqrt{10},\\qquad2b=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088323 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8ac809353e7834d328d645fafb5c02e17386ba087e778a841865ef6313893e79.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088324 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2c35e72acad77ae747c6a78c9794e9b076c047891d4b12d5c9b3176bdc6dde5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088325 :question/problem "![](images/df/dfda198c093371945608318353c997435f2d388ca62f94cc3d27f49cfaf14cb8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $\\pm3$ and the $y$-axis at $\\pm4$. Thus the horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $3$ and $4$, respectively. Its equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3^2}+\\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088354 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088355 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d63963154e858da324418ef8f29aa8e4f309355b7cd6ad105a89f96506027248.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(-1, 2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1, 2)$?" :question/worked-solution "Translate the standard radius-$2$ circle to center $(-1,2)$:\n$$x=2\\cos t-1,\\qquad y=2\\sin t+2.$$\nAt $t=0$ it starts at $(1,2)$ and moves counterclockwise."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088362 :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/25/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088368 :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecd105502fc276297713d3a105db0eb099e8f369e3f4773a78412948ad0870c7.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 4\\cos θ, y = 4\\sin θ.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $θ$?" :question/worked-solution "The displayed arc lies in the third quadrant, from $(-4,0)$ to $(0,-4)$. In $x=4\\cos\\theta$, $y=4\\sin\\theta$, these endpoints occur at $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=3\\pi/2$, respectively. Use $\\pi\\le\\theta\\le3\\pi/2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092059 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left( 6,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(3, 3\\sqrt{3}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092060 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $135^{\\circ}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2, \\quad \\theta=135^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=2 \\cos\\left( 135^{\\circ} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= -\\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\sin\\left(135^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= \\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(  -\\sqrt{2},  \\sqrt{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(r, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} \\right)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $\\left(5r,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tr &= \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2}\\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(5(2\\sqrt{3}),\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\left(10\\sqrt{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= -15\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=5\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left( -15,  5\\sqrt{3}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092081 :question/problem "![](images/83/834eff403ac2a6f696e104ec914cac1b951f8a6d989e11c0e9f2576ac9e7f0d8.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})={t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{({t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092082 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092093 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bb9e5e7315d4bcd5fc21700fdd2597f6d9b585a109145de53c6f94e9530eabf.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092112 :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and $m \\angle AOB=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$\n\n![](images/a0/a0d06318ba26eee754bc3581f5a0f1c126eb0723f8aea2cac0fad075ee56e7f9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right),$ we must have $r=3.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $3,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $3.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n\\theta_B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092113 :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](images/48/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092125 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5647c0f19727d1e626e637464cc57444e0bc0f2f8e99a4afd15d3c3631dc3f0.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi,$ shown in blue in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}+(-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+49{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+1+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092471 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac040bc2c3eb943f947f34602c7c03e90f18e129a0fa7749d8ae60faaab12f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092472 :question/problem "![](images/51/5117030d23370b2167513b7a2eee5258aae4eb9f45ad428fd82e4334c4ce6638.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1+3t,\\,y=2{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092571 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092573 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 1 + \\sin (4t), y = 4\\cos (t)\n$$\nfrom $t = 0$ to $t = 2$?" :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, $$L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\,dt.$$ Here $x'=4\\cos4t$ and $y'=-4\\sin t$. Therefore $$L=4\\int_0^2\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2t}\\,dt.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092574 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{2} + 1, y = t^{3} + 2t\n$$\nfrom $t =-1$ to $t = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate: $x'=2t$ and $y'=3t^2+2$. Thus $$(x')^2+(y')^2=4t^2+(3t^2+2)^2=9t^4+16t^2+4.$$ Therefore $$L=\\int_{-1}^1\\sqrt{9t^4+16t^2+4}\\,dt.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092575 :question/problem "![](images/80/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092597 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092703 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r, \\frac{π}{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r, \\frac{π}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{10}, \\sqrt{10})$." :question/worked-solution "The radius of $A$ is $r=\\sqrt{10+10}=2\\sqrt5$. Thus $B$ has radius $2r=4\\sqrt5$ and angle $\\pi/3$, giving $$B=(4\\sqrt5\\cos(\\pi/3),4\\sqrt5\\sin(\\pi/3))=(2\\sqrt5,2\\sqrt{15}).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092704 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2, θ)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(1, θ - \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$." :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of $A$ give $\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt2/2$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\sqrt2/2$, so $\\theta=-\\pi/4$ modulo $2\\pi$. The angle of $B$ is $-\\pi/2$ modulo $2\\pi$, and its radius is $1$. Therefore $B=(0,-1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092705 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8fd52f3c965f041ea3e9bbc4e8a9705c49ec95be105653d869a0443fa97fac9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(8, \\frac{π}{4})$ and $m∠AOB = \\frac{5π}{12}$, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram puts $B$ counterclockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $\\pi/4+5\\pi/12=2\\pi/3$ and its radius is $8$. Therefore $$B=(8\\cos(2\\pi/3),8\\sin(2\\pi/3))=(-4,4\\sqrt3).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092706 :question/problem "![](images/ee/eed8bcd0866e87fabdb6a87b8a0c4622c61871222c46e7b616927fc3ced4c129.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6, 210^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB = 75^{∘}$, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram puts $B$ clockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $210^\\circ-75^\\circ=135^\\circ$ and its radius is $6$. Thus $$B=(6\\cos135^\\circ,6\\sin135^\\circ)=(-3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092764 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092826 :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c96a0fc119984b83ba68a0cfc50540563a3ea76cc887d3e184238250773e91b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{2} + 2t + 1, y = \\frac{2}{3}t^{3} - 2t\n$$\nfrom $t =-3$ to $t = 2$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate: $x'=2t+2$, $y'=2t^2-2$. Their squared sum is $$4(t+1)^2+4(t^2-1)^2=4(t^4-t^2+2t+2).$$ Hence $$L=2\\int_{-3}^2\\sqrt{t^4-t^2+2t+2}\\,dt.$$ The square root keeps the speed nonnegative on the entire interval, including where $t+1$ changes sign."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092856 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4, \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\pi/4$, both coordinates equal $4\\sqrt2/2=2\\sqrt2$. Thus $P=(2\\sqrt2,2\\sqrt2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092857 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(8, 240^{∘})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "With $r=8$ and $\\theta=240^\\circ$, $$P=(8\\cos240^\\circ,8\\sin240^\\circ)=(-4,-4\\sqrt3).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092858 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $330^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092922 :question/problem "![](images/08/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092923 :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa58c9d1c0680dd94bb25c04e0a268df836e14bfb217f0a20d3bd75224cec4e8.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
